1596 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Consultant: Are we considering only passenger airplanes or all others as well?)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1597 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Interviewer: We are assuming only commercial passenger airplanes.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1598 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Consultant: Is it fair to assume only those airplanes that are currently in use?)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1599 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Interviewer: Sure, let us assume only those airplanes that are in active use.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1600 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Consultant: We can split the world into five continents, leaving out Africa and Antarctica as they have comparatively fewer airports and commercial flights than other continents.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1601 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Interviewer: Okay, you may go ahead with the guesstimate for any one continent, say North America.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1602 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Consultant: I would like to continue with a demand side approach. Broadly, I would divide the total number of passengers in the country traveling by flights by the average number of passengers in a commercial flight. This would give me the average number of flights per year. I can divide this number by the average number of flights flown by an airplane in a year.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1603 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <496E7465727669657765723A2054686973207365656D73206C696B65206120766961626C6520617070726F6163682E204E6F77206C65749073206469766520696E746F207468652064657461696C732E>
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1604 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Consultant: I would like to continue with the USA alone and then multiply this number by 0.1 for Canada due to its reduced population and 0.1 for Mexico due to lower standards of living resulting in fewer people having access to air travel. So overall multiply the number of airplanes in the USA by 1.2.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1605 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Interviewer: Those seem like reasonable assumptions.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1606 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Consultant: We can split the total population into three categories - those who travel 1-2 times a year for leisure, those who travel 3-4 times a year for leisure and those who travel for work on average 4-6 times a year. Assuming one-third of the population can afford air travel for leisure in some capacity, 25% comes under the first)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
