<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (category while 5% come under the second category. Additionally, around 10% will travel regularly for work. This gives us 300,000,000*[0.25*1+0.05*3+0.1*5] = 270,000,000 passengers. Also, an average commercial flight will carry 200 passengers. This gives 270000000/200 = 1350000 flights per year.)
/Pg 56 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1620 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Interviewer: That seems fair.)
/Pg 56 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1621 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Consultant: We can assume a plane flying domestically flies up to 4 flights per day while those flying internationally fly 1-2 flights per day. On average let us assume 3 flights per day. This gives us 3*365, which is approximately 1000 flights per year. Finally we get 1350000/1000 = 1350 planes are actively flying every year. If we assume an average lifespan of an airplane to be 20 years, we get 1350/20 = 67.5 planes. Therefore, approximately 70 airplanes are manufactured in the USA every year. This gives 70*1.2 = 84 airplanes manufactured in North America every year.)
/Pg 56 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1622 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Interviewer: This number seems to be correct. Can you quickly give a supply side approach as a sanity check?)
/Pg 56 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1623 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Consultant: Sure, do we know which companies manufacture airplanes in North America?)
/Pg 56 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
