1801 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/T (Consultant: Should we assume passenger and cargo flights? Interviewer: No, consider only passenger flights. Consultant: Is it ok to consider a typical day, that is, no specific seasonal demand? Interviewer: Yes, go ahead. Consultant: We can assume a supply side approach as demand side will require too many assumptions and will not properly take into account supply side bottlenecks Interviewer: Sure, go ahead. Consultant: The only major bottleneck on the number of flights landing is the availability of parking gates in the airport because the time required for preparation and take-off is much larger than that of landing and parking. So let us assume that the number of flights landing is equal to the number of flights taking off. Interviewer: That seems fair. Consultant: The number of flights taking off is determined by the time taken for boarding, preparation and take-off. Let a day into two parts in terms of number of flights - peak and non-peak of 8 hours each. We can also assume the number of boarding gates to be around 50. Interviewer: Ok, so you are assuming that the number of boarding gates is equal to the number of parking gates. Consultant: Yes. Interviewer: Ok, continue.)
/E (Consultant: Should we assume passenger and cargo flights? Interviewer: No, consider only passenger flights. Consultant: Is it ok to consider a typical day, that is, no specific seasonal demand? Interviewer: Yes, go ahead. Consultant: We can assume a supply side approach as demand side will require too many assumptions and will not properly take into account supply side bottlenecks Interviewer: Sure, go ahead. Consultant: The only major bottleneck on the number of flights landing is the availability of parking gates in the airport because the time required for preparation and take-off is much larger than that of landing and parking. So let us assume that the number of flights landing is equal to the number of flights taking off. Interviewer: That seems fair. Consultant: The number of flights taking off is determined by the time taken for boarding, preparation and take-off. Let a day into two parts in terms of number of flights - peak and non-peak of 8 hours each. We can also assume the number of boarding gates to be around 50. Interviewer: Ok, so you are assuming that the number of boarding gates is equal to the number of parking gates. Consultant: Yes. Interviewer: Ok, continue.)
/Pg 64 0 R
/K [0]
>>
