<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/T (Consultant: Let us assume that each plane spends one hour from engines off at the parking gate to take-off. This includes 15 minutes of off-boarding time and 40-45 minutes of boarding time. This one hour also includes cleaning, re-fueling and maintenance. Interviewer: Sure, that seems reasonable. Consultant: For peak hours, there are 50 gates and 12 flights per gate equating to 600 flights. For non-peak hours, we assume 60 percent or 30 gates are in use. So 30 gates and 12 flights per gate equating to 360 flights. This gives us a total of 960 flights per day. Interviewer: This number seems correct. We can close the case here.)
/E (Consultant: Let us assume that each plane spends one hour from engines off at the parking gate to take-off. This includes 15 minutes of off-boarding time and 40-45 minutes of boarding time. This one hour also includes cleaning, re-fueling and maintenance. Interviewer: Sure, that seems reasonable. Consultant: For peak hours, there are 50 gates and 12 flights per gate equating to 600 flights. For non-peak hours, we assume 60 percent or 30 gates are in use. So 30 gates and 12 flights per gate equating to 360 flights. This gives us a total of 960 flights per day. Interviewer: This number seems correct. We can close the case here.)
/Pg 65 0 R
